CloudBurrow patch 20 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1070).

The cost: Analyzer.Analyze (internal/analyzer.go) walked each statement's
parse tree five times before analyzing it: getParameterMode twice (in
sliceArgsPerStatement and again in the statement's action), then
countPositionalParams or collectNamedParamNames, validateLiteralCasts and
preRegisterWildcardTables. Each node of each walk is several calls into the
WebAssembly engine (NumChildren, Child, the node's type), so for an
INSERT ... VALUES of a few thousand literal rows, the batches a CloudBurrow
load sends, the walks were 36% of the statement's CPU time. One walk,
collectStmtASTFacts, now gathers all of it: the `?` count, the @names, the
first CAST(<string literal> AS <numeric type>) that cannot succeed, and the
wildcard table paths. Each is used where it was before and in the same
order (parameter mode, then the cast error, then the wildcard tables are
registered, lazily in the statement's action), so the errors and their
messages are unchanged.

Measured on Apple silicon (M4 Max), 2,000-row INSERT ... VALUES of an INT64,
a FLOAT64 and a STRING: BenchmarkInsertValues 190 to 117 ms per statement
(10,500 to 17,000 rows/s); on the patched emulator built natively,
20,000 rows by sequential jobs.query, 9,900-10,100 to 15,700-16,100 rows/s,
the process flat at about 300 MiB as before. What is left is mostly the
parse (ParseNextScriptStatement, about 39%) and formatting each row's SQL
(about 20%).

